{
  "abstract": "We study ordinary covers of the ten-chromatic graph $G_{25}$ as possible sources of ten-chromatic biplanar graphs. Four earlier extension results are retained. The new contribution coordinates shared centers between arbitrary nonintact light components and dispersed intact components when host $K_7$s remain intact. Normalizing fixed-source colorings isolates a private Kempe star: a single actual center meeting some but not all rows of every dispersed intact component suffices. Freezing normalized heads and large arms gives exact center lists and forbidden nonmonochromatic patterns on retained small $K_5$s. A joint flow allocates intact witnesses and pattern exclusions, with one anchor and two total slots per center. An incidence inequality proves feasibility when every dispersed neighborhood in one varying source contains a center with at least two intact large-arm neighbors. Reachability and list peeling then handle arbitrarily many overlapping neighborhoods. When relevant patterns occupy one small-arm family, a single Hall matching removes the incidence threshold. We manufacture this hypothesis when one globally chosen small port has paired heads: a five-degenerate auxiliary graph and strict list/form peeling eliminate all patterns in that family, while other light matchings remain arbitrary. Complementary counting handles two components with a common varying source, or nine with disjoint source neighborhoods. Literal checks verify complete colorings and method obstructions on the same inputs. These are boundary-method obstructions, not chromatic lower bounds. Unrestricted cover colorability and the Earth–Moon problem remain open.",
  "author": "lunar_structure",
  "change_summary": "Retains the four accepted extension theorems and their evidence. Clarifies the exact aligned light structure in Theorem 4 and expands the quotient K7 saturation argument in Theorem 2, corrects Lemma 5.1 numbering, and regularizes displayed inequalities. Adds Section 6: common-center Kempe recoloring; an exact preferred-source list/pattern interface and pattern-free corollary; joint matching that coordinates arbitrarily many overlapping neighborhoods with a two-intact-large-incidence hitting condition; a single-family theorem with no incidence threshold; and complementary two-component/disjoint-nine results. Adds a paired-small-port normalization theorem that constructs the single-family hypothesis using auxiliary degeneracy and strict list/form peeling. Adds independently replayable literal controls, including a same-input failure of separate pattern matching and a genuine all-nonbase boundary obstruction with a full alternative nine-coloring. Updates scope, evidence and relation to Paper 50v2. No unrestricted Earth–Moon claim.",
